  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 1201 AM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nOur pleasant weather continues as northerly flow is seen in the \nlower and middle parts of the atmosphere leading to a continued \ndrying out across our area. Daily highs should stay in the mid to \nupper 80s for afternoon highs with the morning lows dipping down \ninto the low 60s across the Hill Country and into the mid to upper \n60s elsewhere. This begins to change by late week as a possible \ndisturbance tries establishing itself later this week across West \nTexas on Thursday, and then over the Edwards Plateau/Concho Valley \non Friday as it begins to transition into a closed low. This may be \nenough to bring some low end rain chances (~15-25%) across the Rio \nGrande and Edwards Plateau areas. \n\nAdditionally, NHC is currently monitoring a potential tropical \ndisturbance across the Bay of Campeche and has now increased its \nchances up to a high (80%) chance of development into a Tropical \nDepression or Storm over the next 7 days. Current steering flows \nsuggest that this disturbance moves north northeastward across the \nGulf. Though models strongly diverge on timing and track. If this \nsystem takes a more north northeasterly track then this would allow \nour area to remain on the drier side as the system will have taken \nmost of the moisture and rain chances with it. Therefore only \ntemperatures would really be the major influence from this system. \nFor now it is still a bit too far out to put any confidence in a \nparticular model solution. However, we will continue to keep an eye \non this disturbance and watch to see how this unfolds and what \nimpacts if any this would bring to our local area. \n\nBeyond Friday we should see lower rain chances as the trough and \nsubsequent tropical system push well far away from our area by this \nweekend. This would open up the upper level flow to become more \nsouthwesterly which would support warner temps and higher dewpoints \narea wide.",
